From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Berkay=20K=C3=B6sebay?= <brkosebay@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 17:12:29 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] power: supply: apple_tristar_charger: Host mode for the
 camera adapter

The Lightning to USB camera adapter (digital ID 11 f0) has the iPad act
as USB host. iOS identifies it on every attach, runs the post-ID
exchange with accessory power on, makes the port a host, then agrees on
power over the ID bus. On unplug the port returns to the USB device
controller.

Add that policy to the charger, active only when the device tree names
the port's role switch (apple,usb-role-switch). Every 250 ms it reads
the Tristar status (VBUS, orientation, plug present) inside the role
switch's monitor, so that no role change overlaps the read. With a plug
attached it asks for the digital ID 300 ms after the plug edge and then
every 2 s (10 s while a USB host has configured the gadget). For the
adapter it switches accessory power on, runs the post-ID exchange,
requests the host role, then runs the power handshake once strict VBUS
appears (two tries, 15 s deadline) and keeps the role, re-checking the
ID every 5 s. Unplug detection follows iOS: a fully empty status at
once (three reads); while the adapter is ours, plug-present clear with
VBUS lingering for 2 s (an unplug in host mode can read 0x25);
plug-present back after 0.5 s or more is a replug, which releases the
role and identifies again. A different ID in host mode (a fast swap to
a computer's cable) releases the role as well.

The charger and the policy share the Tristar under the charger's lock.
While the policy holds the port (from the host request until the role is
given back, and around each accessory-power or role change), the charger
does not sample the mux. A charger on the adapter's power port is then
not identified, and the power handshake's state bytes are not decoded,
so the charger takes it as an unidentified source: 500 mA, through its
usual ramp and battery checks. It identifies the source again once the
policy lets go. Role-switch calls are never made with the charger's lock
held, since the role switch calls back into the charger with its own
lock held. The policy stops with the charger's poll on remove, shutdown
and suspend, and gives a held host role back on unbind. The policy looks
the role switch up from its own poll, so charging never waits for the
role switch to bind. The charger owns the device controller's role (off
until the source is identified). While the policy holds the port, that
role stays off even once the source is identified, so the device
controller never pulls up while the PHY serves the host controllers; the
first poll after the policy lets go turns it on, or identifies the
source again if it was the adapter. Stopping the charger always turns it
on.

A board without the role switch gets no new polling and no extra
Tristar traffic; with the option off, the charger is unchanged.

The interface between the policy and the role switch driver, which
implements it, is added here as include/linux/usb/apple-s8000-role.h:
the policy switches accessory power and lets the role switch sample the
Tristar status. Without the role switch driver the header's stubs return
-EOPNOTSUPP.

Tested on an iPad 5 (J71t) with the Lightning to USB camera adapter.

[Orchard: from orchard PR #126, commit 864b4971; input limit in host
 mode is 500 mA instead of 2.1 A after the power handshake, since the
 source behind the adapter is not identified; the policy is the only
 user of the host role, so the host-entry claim is gone]

